-- §6 C1 AT A RIGID-VARIABLE GOAL -- the goal-site half of C1's requantification,
-- and the shape #1155 acceptance item 2 exists for. This is 1 of 4; the other
-- three are the controls named at the bottom.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §3 `match`, §3 `⊑` and §6 C1 -- NOT captured):
--
--   `firstRow` declares no context, so §4 `gen-sig` does not abstract the residual
--   predicate into a dict parameter (C1's exit (ii) does not apply) and §3's
--   precedence leaves it to `inst`: there is no `Index` dict in scope over `a`, so
--   `assum` is silent. The goal `inst` is handed is
--
--       Index (List (List a)) Int (List a)      with `a` RIGID
--
--   -- CLOSED under §6.2 T3 ("every variable of which is either quantified by the
--   scheme just produced or already ground"), so C1 applies. This is exactly the
--   class the old "for any ground `C τ̄`" wording left out.
--
--   match(IE, that goal) = two instances:
--     * prelude `impl Index (List b) Int b`      φ = {b ↦ List a}
--     * user    `impl Index (List (List b)) k c` φ = {b ↦ a, k ↦ Int, c ↦ List a}
--   (`Index (Array b) Int b` and `Index String Int Char` head elsewhere.)
--
--   ⊑ is one-sided matching head-to-head, so NEITHER is an instance of the other:
--     * prelude ⊑ user would need σ(`List (List b)`) = `List b'` -- impossible.
--     * user ⊑ prelude would need σ(`Int`) = `k` -- impossible, `Int` is a
--       constructor and the specific side's `k` is rigid.
--
--   Two ⊑-incomparable matching instances with nothing ⊑-below both: §6 C1's
--   "ambiguous overlap", so THE PROGRAM IS REJECTED. Coherence cannot reach this
--   -- its input is USER decls only, and there is one user impl.
--
-- BEFORE #1155 this printed `[1, 2]` at exit 0 on both engines: the prelude won
-- on declaration index, in silence. The competing impls are named in the message.
--
-- CONTROLS: s6-c1-rigid-goal-unique-min-control (adding the ⊑-minimum makes it
-- ACCEPT and the USER impl win -- so the goal really does reach min⊑ rather than
-- being answered by `assum` or by "prelude always wins");
-- s6-c1-rigid-goal-no-user-impl-baseline (the singleton matching set, `[1, 2]`);
-- s6-c1-rigid-goal-no-call-discriminator (`main` never calls `firstRow`, so the
-- ONLY goal posed is the definition-site RIGID one -- which is what makes the word
-- "rigid" in this file an assertion rather than a label).
impl Index (List (List b)) k c where
  index _ _ = panic "user Index fired"

firstRow : List (List a) -> List a
firstRow xss = index xss 0

main = println (firstRow [[1, 2], [3]])
